    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62349,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 94,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Struma ovarii is usually an incidental finding (with peak incidence in the fifth decade of life), but patients may present with signs and symptoms related to a pelvic mass {19471561; 7802134}. Ascites (with or without pleural effusion) is seen in about one&nbsp;third of patients, and hyperthyroidism in &lt;&nbsp;10% {19696610}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:54:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62350,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 94,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Struma ovarii was the most common type of monodermal teratoma (~3%) in a pathological case series {8037292}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:54:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62351,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 94,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:54:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62352,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 94,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "<p><em>BRAF</em> and <em>KRAS</em> mutations and <em>RET</em>/PTC and <em>PAX8</em>-<em>PPARG</em> rearrangements have been identified, respectively, in papillary (including follicular variant) carcinoma and follicular carcinoma arising in struma ovarii {18058267; 17721188; 26362194; 31538107}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:56:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62353,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 94,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Struma ovarii is typically unilateral, measures &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;cm, and has a solid beefy (sometimes nodular) red to brown cut surface resembling normal thyroid or goitre. It may have cysts or (rarely) be entirely cystic {8037292}. A dermoid cyst may be seen {7802134}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:54:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62354,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 94,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Struma ovarii usually resembles normal thyroid tissue, with variably sized follicles (most frequently macrofollicles) lined by cuboidal to flat cells and filled with colloid. Other patterns include solid, trabecular, pseudopapillary, pseudotubular {7802134}, and predominantly cystic. The predominantly cystic pattern mimics a serous cystadenoma, with only rare follicles seen in its wall {8037292}. Hyperplastic changes (proliferative struma) and adenomatous changes may be seen {8253550}. Cytoplasm is typically scant and pale but may occasionally be abundant and clear or oxyphilic. Nuclei are typically bland, round, and small (rarely they may be optically clear), and mitoses are rare. Stroma is typically scant and collagenous but can be prominent and oedematous. Stromal luteinization may be seen peripherally {3804558}. Papillary, follicular (including highly differentiated) {19696610; 19620939; 18753973; 18058267; 17721188; 22288964; 18317221; 8253550; 26374222}, or anaplastic carcinoma may rarely develop {26630220}. Struma ovarii may be seen in association with mature cystic teratoma, carcinoid (typically trabecular), Brenner tumour, or mucinous cystadenoma {22558485; 30134344; 7802134}. Tumour cells express thyroglobulin, TTF1, and PAX8 {17285841; 16512597}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T16:48:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
